#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Chapter 6 speaker notes (reader-facing, 60-140 words, full sentences, no analogy): {slide: text}. Used by sen0401_ch06_deck_standard_v1_0_0.py."""
__version__ = "1.0.0"
NOTES = {
1: "This chapter reads one real transaction, Alice's payment to Bob, from its first byte to its last, and recomputes every number on the way. The transaction is 194 bytes in the form that travels on the network and 125 bytes in the legacy form. The figure on this slide is a byte map of a transaction from Mastering Bitcoin, third edition, published under the CC BY-SA 4.0 licence. Every code box in the deck runs under Python 3.14.6 and needs no Bitcoin Core node, because the saved transactions and one saved explorer record are supplied with the course page.",
2: "The route has nine numbered stops. The first holds the questions and the concept map, and the remaining eight follow the order of the bytes of a transaction: what a transaction is, version, marker and flag, inputs, the sequence field, outputs, witnesses, lock time with the coinbase, and weight with the foundations. Each number in brackets is a slide that can be opened directly. The three counters on the right give the size of the deck: 71 slides, four true stories with a source link each, and 18 code examples, each with a picture of its result.",
3: "The chapter answers seven competency questions, and each one is answered on a slide that is named in the closing summary. They ask what a transaction asks full nodes to do, how it is serialized in the extended and legacy formats, what version, marker and flag tell a node, which three meanings the sequence field has carried, why witnesses made the identifier malleable, how weight and virtual bytes are computed so that 569 reproduces from the bytes, and who creates new bitcoin and when it can be spent. Readers can use the list as a self-test at the end.",
4: "The concept map divides the chapter into eight branches and 107 concepts. What a transaction is has 10, version, marker and flag has 8, inputs 18, the sequence field 13, outputs 14, witnesses 20, lock time and the coinbase 12, and weight and foundations 12. Witnesses is the largest branch because the chapter spends the most effort on why identifiers were malleable and how segregated witness removed the problem. Every later slide belongs to one of these branches, and the same map is available on the course page, where each concept can be opened.",
5: "This part opens one real transaction and reads it as 194 bytes. Its two tiles name the two ideas of the part. The idea of a transaction is that it names coins to spend and creates new coins, and that every full node checks it before relaying it. The serialization formats are the two ways the same payment is written as bytes: the extended form that carries a witness, and the legacy form that omits it. The table at the bottom shows the seven fields in their fixed order and marks where this part sits among them.",
6: "The two strips show the same payment in its two serializations, drawn to scale so that the width of each box is its size in bytes. The long strip is the extended form, 194 bytes: version, marker and flag, one input of 42 bytes, outputs of 75 bytes, a witness of 67 bytes and a lock time of four bytes. The short strip is the legacy form of 125 bytes. It leaves out the marker, the flag and the witness, which are 69 bytes in all, and these 125 bytes are exactly what the transaction identifier hashes.",
7: "Four ideas open the chapter. A transaction is a request to every full node to update its record of unspent coins. That record is the set of unspent outputs that each node keeps in its own copy. A transaction has five top-level fields, plus two more in the segwit format. The serialized transaction is the string of 194 bytes that Bitcoin Core returned for Alice's payment. The lower line of each card gives a detail of that transaction, so the cards can be checked against the byte map that follows.",
8: "The library parses Alice's transaction from its hexadecimal text into named fields and then adds up the length of each field group. The result is a dictionary: version 4 bytes, marker 1, flag 1, input 42, output 75, witness 67 and lock time 4. The last line sums these values and returns 194, which shows that no byte of the transaction is left unnamed. The chart on the right draws the same numbers, so the outputs and the witness stand out as the two largest groups.",
9: "Four more ideas complete the vocabulary of the first part. The extended serialization format begins with a marker of 00 and a flag of 01 and ends with the witness before the lock time, so it is 69 bytes longer than the legacy form. The legacy format has no marker, flag or witness, and the transaction identifier hashes exactly these bytes. A partially signed bitcoin transaction is a template that programs pass between them, as defined in BIP174. The byte map lists every field with its offset in the serialized transaction.",
10: "The code serializes Alice's transaction twice, once with the witness and once without, and hashes each form. The sizes are 194 and 125 bytes, and the two hashes differ completely: the witness identifier begins f7cdbc7c and the transaction identifier begins 46620030. The table on the right shows the same values. The difference of 69 bytes is the marker, the flag and the witness. This is the first appearance of an idea that returns in the part on witnesses: the txid ignores the witness, and the wtxid covers every byte.",
11: "On 11 January 2009 the cryptographer Hal Finney posted two words, \"running bitcoin\", and on 12 January Satoshi Nakamoto sent him 10 bitcoins. The payment went into block 170 and is the first transaction ever to spend an output, because its single input points at the coinbase of block 9. Its two outputs pay 10 BTC to Finney and return 40 BTC to the sender. It is a legacy transaction with no marker, no flag and no witness, so the format that this chapter reads is the same one that was used in block 170. The source link on the slide opens the transaction in an explorer.",
12: "The first six bytes of a transaction do not carry money; they tell a node which rules apply and whether a witness follows. The version field takes four bytes and says which edition of the rules the transaction uses. The marker and the flag take one byte each and announce the extended format. This part reads those six bytes in Alice's transaction, shows how an old parser and a new parser treat them differently, and explains the versions that exist. The table at the bottom marks the first two fields among the seven.",
13: "The six cells are the first six bytes of Alice's transaction: four bytes of version, one byte of marker and one byte of flag. The version is written little-endian, so the number 1 appears as 01 00 00 00 with the smallest byte first. A legacy parser reads the following 00 as an input count of zero, finds what looks like an empty transaction and stops. A segwit parser sees the marker 00 and the flag 01, knows that a witness follows and then reads the real input count. No real transaction has zero inputs, so the zero acts as a signal.",
14: "Three version values matter in this part. Version 1 is the original format and is written 01000000 in the first four bytes, as in Alice's transaction. Version 2 is the version that BIP68 uses: from version 2 on, the sequence field of each input can carry a relative timelock. Version 3 is a proposal about relay policy, which means nodes may choose to relay such transactions under stricter rules, but it is not a change to consensus. Each card gives the meaning first and then one fact that can be checked in the chapter.",
15: "The code reads the first six bytes of Alice's transaction. Line 2 reads four bytes as a little-endian integer and returns version 1. Line 3 reads the next two bytes and returns marker 0 and flag 1. The hexadecimal text of the six bytes is 010000000001, shown in the table on the right with the field name and value of each byte group. A zero where an input count would be expected tells a node that the extended format follows, and the node then reads the witness after the outputs.",
16: "Three more cards finish the first group. A presigned transaction is signed now and broadcast later, which is why its signatures must already exist when it is created. The marker byte is a zero in the position where an input count is expected; since a transaction cannot have zero inputs, the zero is unambiguous. The flag byte is one today, and other values are reserved for later formats. The marker and the flag are sometimes called the extended marker and flag in the literature, and both are left out of the txid.",
17: "Inputs name coins and never state an amount. This part explains the four ideas that describe an input: the input list, the outpoint, byte order and the input script. The list holds every coin that the transaction spends, and Alice's transaction has one. The outpoint is the address of a coin, made of an earlier txid and an output number. Byte order matters because the same 32 bytes are stored in one order and printed by explorers in the reverse. The input script is the slot where legacy inputs put their proof, and it is empty in segwit inputs.",
18: "An input is only an address: the txid of an earlier transaction and an output number counted from zero. In the drawing, the earlier transaction has two outputs, of 18,210,494 and 100,000 satoshis, and Alice's input points at output 1, the coin of 100,000. The input itself states no amount. The right side shows CompactSize, the encoding of counts: values up to 252 take one byte, and a first byte of fd, fe or ff warns that two, four or eight more bytes follow, which makes three, five or nine bytes in all.",
19: "These cards give the input count, the variable-length integer, the outpoint and a related name. The input count is at least one in a valid transaction, and it is written in CompactSize. CompactSize takes one, three, five or nine bytes depending on its value. Other encodings of integers exist in Bitcoin, and this name is the one used for counts in transactions. The outpoint is 36 bytes: 32 for the txid and four for the output index. In Alice's transaction the count is 01, because there is one input.",
20: "A count in a transaction takes one byte up to 252 and then three, five or nine bytes. The code encodes six test values chosen at the edges where the length changes: 1, 252, 253, 65,535, 65,536 and 2 to the power of 32. The sizes are 1, 1, 3, 3, 5 and 9 bytes. The first bytes written are 01, fc, fd, fd, fe and ff, and the last three, fd, fe and ff, announce that a longer number follows. The chart on the right draws the same sizes.",
21: "An outpoint is a transaction identifier plus an output index, and the cards define the two parts and two rules about them. The previous transaction's identifier is the first 32 bytes of the outpoint and, in display order, it equals the earlier transaction's txid. The output index takes four bytes and counts from zero; Alice's input spends output 1, the coin of 100,000 satoshis. The rule against double spending says that an output is spent at most once, and two conflicting transactions are two spenders of the same outpoint, of which only one can be confirmed.",
22: "The code reads the outpoint of Alice's single input. Lines 1 and 2 take the stored transaction identifier and the output number. Lines 3 and 4 convert the stored bytes to the display order that explorers print and back again, and the round trip restores the original bytes. The stored form begins eb3ae38f27191aa5 and the printed form begins 4ac5418026798669, which are mirror images. The check returns True because the printed form equals the txid of the earlier transaction, and the index 1 says which of its outputs is spent.",
23: "These cards connect the outpoint to the node's database. The UTXO database holds only unspent outputs: a node removes what a transaction spends and adds what it creates, and Alice's transaction removes one coin and adds two. A digest is a 32-byte value that identifies a transaction; it is the output of a double SHA-256. Internal byte order is the order in which the hash function produces the bytes, and display byte order is the reversed order that explorers print. Mixing them up is a common source of errors when parsing transactions.",
24: "A full node keeps only unspent outputs, and spending changes that set in two ways. Before Alice's payment the set holds the one coin of 100,000 satoshis that her input names. The transaction removes that coin and adds the two coins its outputs create, of 20,000 and 75,000 satoshis. The difference, 100,000 minus 95,000, leaves 5,000 satoshis, which is the fee. No field of the transaction states the fee: it exists only as the amount that the outputs leave unclaimed, and the miner collects it.",
25: "The code replays the spend on a small set of unspent outputs. Lines 1 to 3 build the set before the payment, which holds the single coin of 100,000 satoshis that Alice spends. Lines 4 to 7 remove the coin named by each input and add one coin for each output. After the update the sorted values are 20,000 and 75,000. Lines 8 and 9 compute the fee as the sum before minus the sum after, which is 5,000 satoshis. The table on the right lists the same steps.",
26: "On 18 May 2010 the programmer Laszlo Hanyecz offered 10,000 bitcoins on the bitcointalk forum to anyone who would send him two pizzas. Four days later, on 22 May, he reported that the forum user jercos had ordered them from a Papa John's on Atlantic Avenue in Jacksonville, Florida. The payment confirmed in block 57,043 with 131 inputs, because his wallet held mining rewards of 50 BTC each, and with one output of 10,000 BTC. The remaining 0.99 BTC was the fee. The lesson is that a wallet must list every coin it spends, so one payment can have many inputs.",
27: "The last two cards of the inputs part concern the input script. In a legacy spend the input script carries the proof and takes 107 bytes in the example of the chapter; in a segwit spend the script is empty and the proof is in the witness. The script length prefix tells a parser how many bytes follow: the value 0x6b is 107 in decimal. A parser that reads the prefix first can skip the script without understanding it, which is why the length comes before the content.",
28: "One four-byte field of every input, the sequence field, has carried three different meanings. It was first used for sequence replacement, where a later version of an unfinished transaction with a higher number replaced an earlier one. It later became the signal for opt-in replace-by-fee in BIP125, and then a relative timelock in BIP68. This part reads the 32 bits of the field, decodes four typical values and explains each meaning. The table at the bottom marks the sequence field in the list of seven fields.",
29: "The 32 bits of the sequence field are divided into three groups. Bit 31 is the disable flag: when it is set, there is no relative timelock. Bit 22 is the type flag: clear means the value counts blocks, and set means units of 512 seconds. Bits 15 to 0 hold the value, at most 65,535, which is about a year of blocks. The field is written to the wire as four bytes with the lowest byte first. The three boxes on the right give the three meanings in the order in which they appeared.",
30: "Four cards open the sequence field. The sequence number is the last four bytes of an input, and 0xffffffff means final. Setup and refund transactions form a deposit that needs both signatures, and the refund is signed before the deposit is broadcast. A payment channel exchanges many signed versions off the chain, and only one of them is ever broadcast. The replacement bandwidth attack is the reason the first use of the field was switched off: a single transaction of 20 KB could cause about 10 GB of relay traffic every minute.",
31: "One field has three meanings: final, replaceable and timelocked. The code decodes four typical sequence values with the rules of BIP68 and BIP125. The value 0xffffffff is final, 0xfffffffe is almost final and not replaceable, 0xfffffffd signals replacement, and the value 30 signals replacement and carries a relative timelock of 30 blocks. The result table shows the three columns, and only the last row has a timelock. Bit 31 is the bit that decides whether the lower bits are read as a timelock at all.",
32: "Four cards cover replacement and the relative timelock. Opt-in replace-by-fee lets a transaction that pays a higher fee rate replace one that pays a lower rate, and the replacement must pay more. The BIP125 signal is any sequence below 0xfffffffe, and 0xfffffffd is the usual value. The BIP68 relative timelock with a value of 30 means that the coin must be at least 30 blocks old, so the spend can be confirmed 29 blocks after the block of the output. The type flag at bit 22 chooses blocks when clear and units of 512 seconds when set.",
33: "To demand a wait of one hour, the type flag is set and the value counts units of 512 seconds. The code builds the sequence for 3,600 seconds, which rounds up to eight units and is written 0x400008, and decodes it as 4,096 seconds. Line 3 computes the earliest block for a coin confirmed at block 774,958 with a wait of 30 blocks, which is block 774,988. The last lines print the three masks: 0x80000000 for the disable flag, 0x400000 for the type flag and 0xffff for the value. Together they split the 32 bits into two flags and a 16-bit value.",
34: "The last two cards of the sequence field complete the layout. The disable flag is bit 31: when it is set, the relative timelock is switched off and the other bits are ignored, so the transaction can be confirmed at once. The 16-bit mask keeps only the lowest sixteen bits as the value, which allows 65,535 blocks or units, about a year when counted in blocks. Together with the type flag, they show how one field holds a switch, a unit and a number without any extra bytes.",
35: "Outputs are a list of new coins, each made of an amount and a lock. This part covers the output list, the dust policy and the output script. The output list holds the coins that the transaction creates, and Alice's transaction has two: a payment to Bob and her change. The dust policy keeps tiny coins from filling every node's database. The output script is the lock that decides who may spend the new coin. The part ends with the block that created 184 billion bitcoins and with the dust limits by output type.",
36: "The bar is the spent coin of 100,000 satoshis. Of this, 20,000 go to Bob, 75,000 come back to Alice as change, and 5,000 are left over as the fee. No output claims the fee, so the miner of the block collects it. The lower drawing opens output 0 byte by byte: eight bytes of amount, which read 20,000, one length byte of 22, which is 34 in decimal, and a lock of 34 bytes that begins 51 20 and says who may spend the coin. A bitcoin is 100 million satoshis.",
37: "Four cards define the first fields of an output. The output count is two in Alice's transaction, a payment and change. The amount takes eight bytes, little-endian, in satoshis, and Bob's amount is written 20 4e 00 00 00 00 00 00, which is 20,000. A satoshi is one hundred-millionth of a bitcoin, so 20,000 satoshis are 0.0002 bitcoin. The consensus range of an amount runs from zero to 2.1 quadrillion satoshis, which is 21 million bitcoin, and consensus rejects any amount outside it.",
38: "Outputs state amounts and an input states none, so the code reads each amount from the outputs and the funding amount from the coin that is spent. Line 1 collects the output amounts, 20,000 and 75,000. Line 2 reads the 100,000 satoshis of the coin that Alice spends. Lines 3 to 5 compute the fee as the difference and list the three parts, 20,000, 75,000 and 5,000. No field holds a balance or a fee; both are differences, which the three bars on the right make visible.",
39: "Four cards concern small outputs and the output script. An uneconomical output is worth less than the fee needed to spend it, and relay policy refuses to carry it. The dust limit is 546 satoshis for a legacy output, derived from 182 bytes at the relay fee of 3,000 satoshis per kilovirtual byte; Bob's taproot output needs 330. A data-carrier output uses OP_RETURN, which cannot be spent, so it is never dust. Bob's output script is 34 bytes and begins 51 20, a witness version 1 program, while the change script is 22 bytes.",
40: "In August 2010 two outputs of about 92 billion bitcoins each added up to a negative number in 64-bit arithmetic. The code uses the amount of each output, which is 9,223,372,036,854,277,039 satoshis, doubles it, and wraps the result the way a signed 64-bit integer does, which gives a total of minus 997,538. The check on the total, which asks whether the total is at most the maximum money, returns True. The check on each output returns False and refuses the block. The lesson is to check each amount and the total, because a check on the total alone was not enough.",
41: "On 15 August 2010 block 74,638 contained a transaction with two outputs of 92 billion bitcoins each, 184 billion in all. The amounts were added as 64-bit integers and wrapped around to a small negative number, so the check that outputs do not exceed inputs passed. Jeff Garzik reported the strange block on the forum within two hours, and Satoshi Nakamoto patched the software within about five. The honest chain overtook the bad one at block 74,691 and erased the transaction. Bitcoin Core's tx_check.cpp still cites the bug as CVE-2010-5139.",
42: "The last three cards of the outputs part set the limits of a lock. A script size limit is zero at the minimum and 10,000 bytes at most when a script is spent. An anyone-can-spend script is an empty script that OP_TRUE satisfies, because OP_TRUE leaves a one on the stack and any spender succeeds, so coins locked this way can be taken by anybody. Standard transaction outputs are the few script templates that relay policy allows by default, and a node relays only these templates unless it is configured otherwise.",
43: "Dust is an output that costs more to spend than it is worth. The code builds four output scripts, three that can be spent and one data carrier, and asks the library for the dust threshold of each at the relay fee of 3,000 satoshis per kilovirtual byte. The limits are 546 satoshis for a legacy script, 294 for a segwit version 0 key script, 330 for a taproot script and zero for OP_RETURN. Dust is a policy about who pays for the set of unspent outputs, which everyone stores for ever.",
44: "A witness is the evidence that the spender may spend, kept apart from the identifier of the transaction. This part covers four ideas. The idea of a witness explains what it proves. Malleability is the ability to change a transaction's identifier without changing what it pays. Segwit is the upgrade that moved the proof out of the identifier in 2017. The witness layout is how the separate proofs are written after the outputs and before the lock time. The table at the bottom marks the witness field among the seven.",
45: "The drawing compares the two designs of the identifier. Before segwit the signature sat inside the input script, so the txid covered it, and a stranger who re-encoded the signature changed the txid without changing the payment. After segwit the signature lives in the witness, outside the txid, which covers only version, inputs with empty scripts, outputs and lock time. The wtxid covers every byte, including the marker, the flag and the witness. The txid therefore no longer moves when a signature is re-encoded.",
46: "Four cards introduce the witness. A witness is a value that solves a script: the value 2 solves the script 2 OP_ADD 4 OP_EQUAL. A public key and a signature work as a witness for a problem that only the key holder can solve. Contract protocols need transactions that are signed out of order. A circular dependency arises when the refund transaction needs the deposit's txid first, because before segwit that txid depended on signatures that did not exist yet, so the refund could not be signed safely.",
47: "One small number can be pushed on the script stack in four ways, and each way gives a different txid for the same payment. The code builds the four encodings of the number 2: 52, 0102, 020200 and 4c0102. Lines 2 to 4 place each encoding into the input script of the first-ever spend and compute the txid, and the four identifiers begin 0423a53a, b3485f99, 18da36d1 and 4d986cd8. Four distinct values confirm the result. A stranger could change the txid without changing the payment.",
48: "Four cards describe malleability. Third-party malleability means that a stranger changes the txid but not the payment, for example by choosing another push encoding of the same number. Alternative push encodings are four byte strings for the number 2. Second-party malleability means that a counterparty who holds a valid key re-signs and so changes the txid. Wanted malleability is a mutation that the sender allows, for instance by signing with a flag that permits later changes. Only the first kind is fixed completely by moving the signature out of the txid.",
49: "The code changes only the witness. Line 1 replaces the signature by 65 zero bytes. Lines 2 and 3 compare the identifiers of the changed and the original transaction: the txid is the same, and the wtxid is different. The two Booleans are True and False. The twelve hex digits of each identifier, 466200308696 for the txid and f7cdbc7cf8b9 and 06f7a607a82e for the two wtxids, fill the table. What a hash covers decides what can safely be built on it.",
50: "On 7 February 2014 Mt. Gox, then the largest bitcoin exchange, led by Mark Karpeles in Shibuya, Tokyo, halted withdrawals. On 10 February it blamed transaction malleability for missing bitcoins: a stranger can re-encode an input script and change the txid, and software that tracks payments by txid then believes the payment failed. Christian Decker and Roger Wattenhofer of ETH Zurich had recorded a year of network traffic and found that only 1,811 BTC were affected before the press release. On 28 February the exchange filed for bankruptcy protection, 850,000 BTC short.",
51: "Four cards explain how segwit could be introduced without splitting the network. Segregated witness keeps the witness out of the txid. A hard fork is a change that old nodes would reject, so every node must upgrade. A soft fork makes new nodes reject more and accept nothing new, so old nodes still follow the chain; segwit was activated in 2017 as a soft fork. A witness program is a version number followed by a program of two to 40 bytes, and version 0 programs have 20 or 32 bytes.",
52: "Four cards describe the layout of the witness. The witness structure is the new field that carries what the input script held. It sits between the outputs and the lock time. A witness stack exists for each input, counted by the number of inputs, so Alice's transaction has one stack. A witness item is length-prefixed; Alice's has one item of 65 bytes, a signature, and the whole witness takes 67 bytes with the item count and length. A legacy input's witness is a stack of zero items, written as a single 00 byte.",
53: "Segregated witness was a soft fork, which is why it could be activated without splitting the network. A hard fork loosens the rules: blocks that old nodes reject become valid, so every node must upgrade or the chain splits. A soft fork tightens the rules: new nodes reject more, and they accept nothing new, so old nodes still follow the chain and accept its blocks. Segwit moved the signature to a place that old nodes treat as spendable by anyone and that new nodes check strictly. A soft fork can only restrict, and old nodes keep accepting the blocks.",
54: "This part covers two special cases. Lock time is a rule that a transaction may not enter a block before a given time, and it is the last field of a transaction. Coinbase transactions are the first transaction of every block and the only ones that create new coins; their single input has a null outpoint. The part shows how the lock time field is read as a height or as a time, how the median time past is used to compare it, how the subsidy halves, and why a coinbase coin can be spent only after 100 blocks.",
55: "The two pictures show two ways of waiting. The number line shows how one lock time field is read: zero means any block, values from 1 to 499,999,999 are a block height, and values from 500,000,000 up are a time in seconds. The value 123,456 means block 123,456 or later, and 1,675,556,500 means 5 February 2023, tested against the median of eleven block times. The second picture shows coinbase maturity: a coin made in block 9 stays locked through block 108 and may be spent from block 109, when its age is 100.",
56: "Four cards cover the lock time field. The field has four bytes, and zero means that the transaction is eligible for any block. As a block height, 123,456 means block 123,456 or later. As an epoch time, a value of 500,000,000 or more is a timestamp in seconds, and 1,675,556,500 is 5 February 2023. The median time past is the middle of the last eleven block times; a time-based lock time is compared with it and not with the newest block timestamp, because timestamps are not monotonic.",
57: "Block timestamps are not monotonic, so a lock time is compared with the median of the last eleven block times. The code builds eleven times out of order, offsets from 1,675,556,500, and computes the median time past, which is 2,100 seconds after the origin and 600 seconds below the highest value. The last lines classify three lock times: 0 is any block, 123,456 is a block height and 1,675,556,500 is an epoch time. A lock time says not before; nothing in a transaction says not after.",
58: "Four cards describe the coinbase. The coinbase transaction is first in the block and is made by the miner; it is also called a generation transaction. Its input points at the null outpoint: 32 zero bytes with index 0xffffffff. The coinbase field takes 2 to 100 bytes and begins with the block height; miners often add a message or a pool tag. The block subsidy began at 50 BTC and halves every 210,000 blocks, so block 210,000 was the first with 25 BTC.",
59: "The subsidy starts at 50 bitcoin and halves every 210,000 blocks, which the library computes with a right shift. The code lists the first nine values: 50, 25, 12.5, 6.25, 3.125, 1.5625, 0.78125, 0.390625 and 0.1953125. The last subsidy is paid at block 6,929,999, after which the subsidy is zero for good. The total ever issued is 20,999,999.9769 bitcoin, just below 21 million. The coinbase is the only transaction that creates bitcoin, and the chart on the right draws the staircase.",
60: "Two cards finish the coinbase. The block reward is the fees plus the subsidy, and it is a ceiling: a miner may claim less, never more. The maturity rule says that a coinbase output needs 100 confirmations before it can be spent, so a coin created in block 9 can be spent from block 109. The reason is that a reorganization could remove the block that created the coin and then every coin spent from it would be invalid; the wait makes that risk very small.",
61: "A coinbase has one null input, and its coins are locked for 100 blocks. The code builds a transaction with a null input, a txid of 32 zero bytes and an output index of 0xffffffff, and tests it with the library. The test returns True for the null input and False for Alice's ordinary transaction. Lines 4 to 6 list the age of a coin created at block 9 for four spend heights: 91 and 99 are too young, and 100 and 161 are mature. The table shows that spending at block 109 is the first allowed.",
62: "The last part closes with what a block is filled by, and with the hashes and rule sets that everything else rests on. Weight and vbytes are the measure that decides how much block space a transaction uses: Alice's 194 bytes weigh 569 units and count as 143 virtual bytes. Foundations are the hash functions, the two identifiers and the rule sets, which separate relay policy from consensus. The table at the bottom marks the whole transaction, since weight covers every field.",
63: "The three bars use the same scale. Alice's transaction has 194 bytes, of which 125 are legacy bytes and 69 are marker, flag and witness. Every byte weighs four units except those 69, which count once, so the weight is 125 times 4 plus 69 times 1, which is 569. Divided by four and rounded up, that is 143 virtual bytes. A block is filled by weight, up to four million units, and the discount on witness bytes is why a segwit transaction is cheaper than the same payment in the legacy form.",
64: "Four cards define weight. Weight is three times the legacy size plus the full size, which is the same as four times the legacy bytes plus one times the marker, flag and witness bytes. Vbytes are weight divided by four and rounded up, so Alice's 569 units are 143 vbytes. The block weight limit is four million weight units, which corresponds to one million vbytes. The weight factors are four for most fields and one for the marker, the flag and the witness, so witness bytes cost a quarter.",
65: "The code computes the weight of each field group of Alice's transaction. Line 1 asks the library for the weight of every field, and lines 2 and 3 add the weights per group. The groups are version 16, marker 1, flag 1, input 168, output 300, witness 67 and lock time 16. The total is 569, which matches the book, and the virtual size is 143. Every field weighs four times its bytes except marker, flag and witness, so the witness is cheap.",
66: "Four cards cover hashing and the two identifiers. A hash function turns any input into a digest of a fixed 32 bytes. Double SHA-256 is SHA-256 applied to the output of SHA-256, and Bitcoin uses it to name a transaction. The transaction identifier is the double SHA-256 of the legacy bytes, reversed for display; Alice's begins 466200308696. The witness transaction identifier hashes everything, including the witness, and Alice's begins f7cdbc7cf8b9. The two identifiers differ whenever a transaction has a witness.",
67: "A txid is not a field of the transaction; it is computed. The code starts from the 125 legacy bytes, the form without the witness. Line 2 applies SHA-256 twice, hashing the first digest again. Line 3 reverses the digest for display, which gives a value beginning 466200308696215b, and compares it with the library's txid, which returns True. The table on the right lists the same steps. Identifiers are computed, never stored, so anyone can recompute them.",
68: "The last two cards separate policy from rules. Relay policy is what a node chooses to relay and keep: it can differ between nodes and changes with each release, and the dust limit is an example. A consensus rule is what every node must enforce identically, and a block that breaks one is rejected by all nodes. A transaction that breaks only policy can still be confirmed if a miner includes it, but few nodes will relay it to that miner.",
69: "The five lines are the chapter in miniature. A transaction asks strangers to update their ledgers: inputs point, witnesses prove and outputs assign. The sequence field has meant three things, and the lock time is read against the median of eleven blocks. An output is an amount and a condition, the fee is a difference, and dust is a policy about everyone's database. Witnesses in the input script made identifiers malleable, and segregated witness moved them out as a soft fork. The coinbase creates coins on a schedule that ends at block 6,929,999, and weight is what a block is filled by.",
70: "Every entry is free to read, and the links were opened on the date in the subtitle. The reading list begins with chapter 6 of Mastering Bitcoin. The standards are BIP 141 for segregated witness and weight, BIP 68 for relative lock time and BIP 125 for opt-in replace-by-fee. The tools are Alice's transaction on mempool.space, in block 775,072, and the Bitcoin Wiki page on transactions. The community links are the bitcointalk threads on the pizza payment and on block 74,638. The last link is a video that walks through inputs, outputs and signatures. The task at the bottom is to parse any transaction and match the explorer's identifier and weight.",
71: "Four claims close the chapter, and each one is carried by a computation that ran on a slide. A transaction asks full nodes to remove the coins its inputs name and add the coins its outputs create. The sequence field and the lock time say what a transaction permits and when. Identifiers were malleable because the signature was inside the txid, and segregated witness moved it out. The weight of 569 and the 143 virtual bytes reproduce from the 194 bytes of Alice's transaction. A reader who can state each claim and point to its slide has met the competency questions of the chapter.",
}
